#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# symmetry_reduce_order

#% text
# `symmetry_reduce_order(eq, y, x, gen, v, r)` reduces the ordinary differential equation `eq` for `y(x)` by its symmetry `gen = [xi, eta]`: it gives `[eq2, r == r(x, y), v == ...]`, where `eq2` is an equation of one order less for `v(r) = ds/dr` in the canonical coordinates `r, s` of `gen`, and the last two elements give `r` and `v` in terms of `x`, `y` and `y'(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The canonical coordinates come from `canonical_coordinates`; `x` and `y` are written in terms of `r` and `s(r)`, the derivatives by the chain rule, and the symmetry makes the result free of `s`. The result is `none` when the coordinates cannot be found or inverted.

#% item
# A solution of the reduced equation gives the solutions of `eq` by one more integration, `s = integrate(v(r), r) + c`; `symmetry_d_solve` does this.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The Ermakov-Pinney equation is invariant under translations in `x`; in the canonical coordinates `r = y`, `s = x` it becomes a first-order equation for `v = dx/dy`:

#% cell
symmetry_reduce_order(y''(x) == y(x)^(-3), y, x, [1, 0], v, r)
#% output
#= [-v(r)^3 - r^3*v'(r) == 0, r == y, v == y'(x)^-1]

#% text
# An equation without `y` by the translation in `y`:

#% cell
symmetry_reduce_order(x*y''(x) + y'(x) == x*y'(x)^3, y, x, [0, 1], v, r)
#% output
#= [r*v'(r) - r*v(r)^3 + v(r) == 0, r == x, v == y'(x)]

#% text
# The scaling of a homogeneous first-order equation turns it into a quadrature:

#% cell
symmetry_reduce_order(y'(x) == (y(x)/x)^2 + y(x)/x, y, x, [x, y], v, r)
#% output
#= [1 - r^2*v(r) == 0, r == y/x, v == x/(-y + x*y'(x))]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `canonical_coordinates`, `symmetry_d_solve`, `lie_point_symmetries`
